<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!--  Chapter 3: Exploring Data                                         -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Data-first chapter. Deliberately light: notice patterns, ask      -->
<!--  questions, read a pivot table. No formal definitions of mean,     -->
<!--  variance, missingness taxonomy, etc. - those are introduced only  -->
<!--  when they are needed, in later chapters:                          -->
<!--  This chapter's job is only to make the reader curious enough to   -->
<!--  want those later chapters.                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-exploring-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exploring Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-exploring-data">
    <p>
      This chapter is a bit different.
      There's no formula to learn, and nothing here is the <q>correct</q> way to
      analyse anything.
      What we want is simpler, and honestly, more important: open the data, look
      at it, and let it surprise you.
      Every method later in this book (cleaning, multidimensional
      visualisation, factors, handling missing values, and measuring
      variability) is there to answer a question that this chapter raises.
      And we're going to deliberately leave those questions open for now!
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 0, DRAFT A (ACTIVE): why explore at all - kenya crash data -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-malaria-map-hook">
    <title>Why Explore At All?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's start with something that affects a lot of people in Kenya: road
        safety.
        Since 2012, the National Transport and Safety Authority (NTSA) has been
        recording road crash data from across the country: the county, the
        road, the time, the type of vehicle, and who was involved.
        Every year, they publish summaries of this on their website.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-crashes">
      <title>Who is dying on Kenya's roads, and where?</title>
      <p>
        The NTSA actually publishes two kinds of record.
        The first is a daily comparative report.
        This gives the number of people killed and injured so far that year,
        split across six types of road user: pedestrians, passengers, drivers,
        pillion passengers, pedal cyclists, and motorcyclists.
        You can see a snippet of one in <xref ref="table-ntsa-daily-report"/>.
      </p>

