      </table>

      <p>
        The second is the fatal accident report, and this one comes in two
        parts.
        The crash-level part has one row for each incident, with the road, the
        place, the vehicles involved, and a short description of what happened
        (see <xref ref="table-ntsa-fatal-report"/>).
        The victim-level part has one row for each person, with their name,
        gender, age, a cause code, and whether they were a driver, a pedestrian,
        and so on.
      </p>

      <p>
        Did you spot it?
        Even in this small snippet, the NTSA data has a multilevel structure:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The yearly data summarises deaths over the year for different
              types of victim (passengers, motorcyclists, and so on).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The crash-level table has one row per incident.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The victim-level table has one row per person.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        A single crash can involve several victims, so the tables are linked,
        but you can't just swap one for the other.
        Count at the crash level and you'll get one answer; count at the victim
        level and you'll get another, and they answer different questions.
        This is exactly the kind of structure we talked about in
        <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
      </p>

